<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zanj</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zanj was the name that medieval Arabic and Persian writers gave to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples of the East African coast, which they called the land of the Zanj. Many Zanj were carried as slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where those forced to clear the salt marshes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> of 869 to 883. The name survives in Zanzibar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zanj.jpg" alt="Zanj" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Runehelmet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swahili_coast.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Slaves were obtained through the trans-Saharan trade, through Mediterranean commerce and from raids on Sicily and Italy; 5,000 black <b>Zanj</b> slaves are recorded as part of the garrison of al-Abbasiyya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The geographer al-Idrisi, writing in the 1150s, mentions Mombasa as a small town on the coast of the <b>Zanj</b>, and by the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries it had become a Muslim trading centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In southern Iraq enslaved Africans, the <b>Zanj</b>, were kept in labour camps to scrape the salty topsoil from the marshes near Basra; in 869 they rose under Ali ibn Muhammad and held out for fourteen years.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>Thousands of enslaved East Africans, called <b>Zanj</b>, were forced to clear the salt-encrusted soil of the marshes around Basra for farming.</blockquote><a href="/state/zanj-state">Zanj state</a></li></ul></section>
